# HCW: Generality of the Invariants

```elixir
project_path = System.get_env("PLUMBLINE_PROJECT_PATH") || Path.join(__DIR__, "..")

Mix.install([{:plumbline, path: project_path}])
```

## What this document is

`hcw_orbital_mechanics.livemd` is the specification. It states the
physics, derives the invariants, and asserts them **at one scenario** —
a 400 km orbit with a deputy offset 1 km radially.

That is a point assertion. The invariants themselves claim more than
that: they are properties that hold for *every* valid state, at *every*
altitude. This notebook asserts the stronger claim, by sampling.

It adds no physics. Every derivation lives in the specification, which
is frozen and is where the mathematics is reviewed. This notebook only
widens the set of inputs those results are checked against, so it is
gated separately and keeps its own history:

```sh
bin/run_checkpoint livebooks/hcw_generality.livemd
elixir bin/checkpoint_history.exs hcw_generality
```

A failure here and a pass in the specification notebook together mean
something specific: the implementation is correct *at the scenario that
was checked* and wrong somewhere else in the input space. That is the
failure mode a fixed-scenario test suite is structurally unable to see.

### On randomness

Sampling makes a run's result depend on which inputs it drew, so the
seed is fixed and recorded in the report. A red run must be repeatable;
an unreproducible failure is barely better than no check at all.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.join(project_path, "livebooks/checkpoint.exs"))

Checkpoint.init([
  "general_I1_periodicity",
  "general_I2_cross_track_decoupling",
  "general_I3_conservation",
  "general_rk4_vs_analytic",
  "general_integrator_independent_of_oracle"
])
```

## Sampling

Altitudes span low Earth orbit; separations stay within the linearised
model's validity envelope — kilometres against an orbital radius of
thousands. Velocities are drawn on the natural scale `n · r`, which is
the speed at which a relative orbit of size `r` is traversed.

```elixir
alias Plumbline.Dynamics.HCW

seed = 20_260_905
:rand.seed(:exsss, {seed, seed, seed})

samples = 100
steps = 2_000

mu = 398_600.4418
earth_radius = 6378.137

# Separation half-range, km.
r_max = 10.0

uniform = fn lo, hi -> lo + :rand.uniform() * (hi - lo) end

# Mean motion for a random LEO altitude between 300 and 1200 km.
sample_n = fn ->
  a = earth_radius + uniform.(300.0, 1200.0)
  :math.sqrt(mu / (a * a * a))
end

sample_state = fn n ->
  v_max = n * r_max

  %HCW.State{
    x: uniform.(-r_max, r_max),
    y: uniform.(-r_max, r_max),
    z: uniform.(-r_max, r_max),
    vx: uniform.(-v_max, v_max),
    vy: uniform.(-v_max, v_max),
    vz: uniform.(-v_max, v_max)
  }
end

# A length scale for the trajectory, so tolerances can be relative.
# Absolute metres mean something different on a 1 km orbit than on a
# 20 km one, and a fixed bound would be either toothless or spuriously
# red depending on the draw.
scale = fn s, n ->
  :math.sqrt(s.x * s.x + s.y * s.y + s.z * s.z) +
    :math.sqrt(s.vx * s.vx + s.vy * s.vy + s.vz * s.vz) / n
end

period_of = fn n -> 2 * :math.pi() / n end

%{seed: seed, samples: samples, steps_per_orbit: steps}
```

## I1 — every drift-free orbit closes, at every altitude

The drift-free condition constrains one velocity component and leaves
the rest of the state free, including cross-track motion, which is
periodic at the same rate. So the specification's periodicity claim
should hold for a whole family of states, not the one it asserts.

```elixir
i1_worst =
  for _ <- 1..samples, reduce: 0.0 do
    worst ->
      n = sample_n.()
      s = sample_state.(n)
      # The only constraint: the drift-free condition of I1.
      s0 = %{s | vy: -2 * n * s.x}

      t = period_of.(n)
      {_, final} = HCW.propagate(s0, n, t / steps, t) |> List.last()

      closure =
        :math.sqrt(
          :math.pow(final.x - s0.x, 2) + :math.pow(final.y - s0.y, 2) +
            :math.pow(final.z - s0.z, 2)
        )

      max(worst, closure / scale.(s0, n))
  end

Checkpoint.check("general_I1_periodicity", i1_worst < 1.0e-6,
  expected: 0.0,
  actual: i1_worst,
  tolerance: 1.0e-6,
  unit: "relative",
  message: "worst relative closure error after one period, over #{samples} random drift-free states and altitudes"
)
```

## I2 — cross-track stays exactly zero, from any in-plane state

```elixir
i2_worst =
  for _ <- 1..samples, reduce: 0.0 do
    worst ->
      n = sample_n.()
      s = sample_state.(n)
      s0 = %{s | z: 0.0, vz: 0.0}

      t = period_of.(n)

      excursion =
        s0
        |> HCW.propagate(n, t / steps, t)
        |> Enum.map(fn {_, p} -> max(abs(p.z), abs(p.vz)) end)
        |> Enum.max()

      max(worst, excursion)
  end

Checkpoint.check("general_I2_cross_track_decoupling", i2_worst == 0.0,
  expected: 0.0,
  actual: i2_worst,
  tolerance: 0.0,
  unit: "km (+ km/s)",
  message: "exact zero required — no arithmetic on an in-plane state can produce cross-track motion"
)
```

## I3 — the integral is conserved on arbitrary states

No constraint at all here: the states drift, tumble, and never close.
Conservation on a periodic orbit would be the weaker claim.

```elixir
i3_worst =
  for _ <- 1..samples, reduce: 0.0 do
    worst ->
      n = sample_n.()
      s0 = sample_state.(n)
      t = period_of.(n)

      js =
        s0
        |> HCW.propagate(n, t / steps, t)
        |> Enum.map(fn {_, p} -> HCW.integral(p, n) end)

      # Normalised by a characteristic energy rather than by J itself:
      # J can pass arbitrarily close to zero on a random state, and
      # dividing by it would manufacture failures out of nothing.
      energy = n * n * :math.pow(scale.(s0, n), 2)

      max(worst, (Enum.max(js) - Enum.min(js)) / energy)
  end

Checkpoint.check("general_I3_conservation", i3_worst < 1.0e-6,
  expected: 0.0,
  actual: i3_worst,
  tolerance: 1.0e-6,
  unit: "relative",
  message: "worst spread of J over one orbit, normalised by n^2 * scale^2, over #{samples} arbitrary states"
)
```

## The oracle, over the whole sampled space

```elixir
deviations =
  for _ <- 1..samples do
    n = sample_n.()
    s0 = sample_state.(n)
    t = period_of.(n)

    s0
    |> HCW.propagate(n, t / steps, t)
    |> Enum.map(fn {sample_t, numeric} ->
      exact = HCW.analytic(s0, n, sample_t)

      :math.sqrt(
        :math.pow(numeric.x - exact.x, 2) + :math.pow(numeric.y - exact.y, 2) +
          :math.pow(numeric.z - exact.z, 2)
      )
    end)
    |> Enum.max()
    |> Kernel./(scale.(s0, n))
  end

worst_deviation = Enum.max(deviations)
least_deviation = Enum.min(deviations)

Checkpoint.check("general_rk4_vs_analytic", worst_deviation < 1.0e-6,
  expected: 0.0,
  actual: worst_deviation,
  tolerance: 1.0e-6,
  unit: "relative",
  message: "worst relative deviation between RK4 and the closed form, over #{samples} arbitrary states"
)
```

Independence has to hold on *every* sampled trajectory, not merely on
average: the smallest per-trajectory deviation is the one that would go
to zero if the integrator and the oracle were the same computation.

```elixir
Checkpoint.check("general_integrator_independent_of_oracle", least_deviation > 0.0,
  expected: "> 0",
  actual: least_deviation,
  unit: "relative",
  message: "smallest per-trajectory deviation across all samples; zero anywhere means propagate/4 and analytic/3 share code"
)
```

```elixir
Checkpoint.complete()
```
